    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d489">

                <pb facs="0679" n="565" xml:id="pg565"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/2858</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ramer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-03-25"><hi rendition="#italic">March 25,
                        1922</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received April 14.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 38</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>:</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Colonel Tracy arrived in Managua March 17th after spending over a month of
                    investigation of conditions on the Nicaraguan-Honduranean frontier. He reported
                    that the revolutionary movement in Honduras is very popular on the Honduranean
                    border and may assume serious proportions at any time; also that the Nicaraguan
                    Government officials in the border districts are giving moral support to this
                    movement by their inactivity …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John E. Ramer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
